# interfaces.md is a promise, not a notepad

A signature recorded here means: this function exists, matches its contract, and
its tests pass. Every later module calls what is written here without checking
the source, so an inaccurate entry propagates into every module built after it.

## Append only after tests pass

Never record a signature for unfinished code. The append is the last step of a
module, after green — not a plan written in advance.

## Record, per public function

- Exact name, exact parameters with types
- Return type **including `| None`** where applicable
- Whether it is `async`
- One line on what it does
- Exceptions that are part of the contract
- Critical constraints — "sole owner of position mutation", "never raises",
  "must be called before the broker"

## Do not record

Private helpers (leading underscore), implementation details, or constants not
meant to be imported elsewhere. This file is the callable surface, nothing more.

## Rules

- **Match the implementation exactly.** If what you built differs from the
  contract in the spec, record what actually exists **and flag the discrepancy** —
  the spec may need amending. Never record the contract's version of a signature
  when the code says something else.
- **Never edit another module's entry.** Entries are appended. Rewriting one
  silently changes what every later module believes it can call.
- **One section per module, in build order**, so the file reads top-to-bottom as
  the dependency chain.
